{
  "slug": "wood-furniture-stripping-and-refinishing",
  "capturedAt": "2026-06-01T15:21:52.952Z",
  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "The first step is identifying the existing finish, because the stripping method depends on it. A ",
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "text": "film-forming finish",
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termId": "cmpv46t1b0000e4v4q1aw2ds9"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": " can be stripped chemically or by heat. A ",
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "text": "penetrating finish",
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termId": "cmpv46t210001e4v49c53u9di"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": " must be sanded back.",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Identifying the existing finish",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Methylated spirit test: dab a small amount on an inconspicuous area and rub with a cloth. If the finish dissolves: shellac. If it softens but does not dissolve: lacquer. If it is unaffected: polyurethane varnish, hard wax oil, or oil.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Acetone test: dissolves nitrocellulose lacquer rapidly. Does not affect polyurethane or hard wax oil.",
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "If sandpaper clogs immediately with a gummy resin: an oil finish is present. Sand the surface lightly to give key before adding a new oil coat.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Stripping options",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Water-based chemical stripper: safer to use than solvent-based; slower acting. Apply generously, cover with cling film to prevent evaporation, leave 2 to 4 hours. Scrape off with a plastic scraper. Works on most film finishes.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Solvent-based stripper: faster, sharper smell. Use outdoors or with good ventilation. Works on thick polyurethane finishes where water-based is slow.",
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Sanding: for thin finishes or oil finishes. Start with 80 grit, progress to 120, then 180. Sand with the grain. Remove all dust before applying new finish.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Choosing the new finish",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Danish or hard wax oil: natural look, easy to apply, maintains with extra coats. Not waterproof; not suitable for table surfaces with hot dishes.",
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Polyurethane varnish: highly durable, waterproof, resistant to heat and abrasion. The default for tables, chairs, and high-use furniture. Available in matte, satin, and gloss.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Milk paint or chalk paint: for decorative, non-structural furniture. Usually needs a wax or varnish top coat for durability.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "title": "Troubleshooter"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "New varnish is bubbling after application"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Bubbles are caused by applying varnish too quickly in direct sunlight or over warm wood. Or by shaking the tin rather than stirring. Sand back lightly to 240 grit, wipe with a tack cloth. And apply the next coat slowly with a quality brush, working with the grain.",
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Method",
            "type": "text",
            "marks": []
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
